# Suppressions for reachable memory that OpenSSL's own internal RSA/BN caches
# hold: RSA blinding state, BN_MONT_CTX Montgomery contexts and similar
# lazily-computed values that RSA key generation and signing fill in. This
# suite exercises both heavily through its own self-signed certificate
# generation, the self-signed path of ctls_ctx_cert_add().
#
# OpenSSL allocates these blocks through its own CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc
# wrappers, and libcrypto's internal static/thread-local state keeps them
# alive for the lifetime of the process. Only an explicit OPENSSL_cleanup()
# call releases them, and we don't make that call: calling it
# unconditionally at process exit isn't safe in a library that is meant to
# be embedded in a larger application, which may have other OpenSSL handles
# in use. So they aren't leaks. They are the same accepted kind of "still
# reachable" library-internal state that tests/chttpclient/ld_so_dlopen.supp
# documents for glibc's own NSS module loading.

{
   openssl_internal_rsa_bn_caches_still_reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   fun:CRYPTO_malloc
   ...
}

# Same kind of state, reached through OpenSSL 3's provider/encoder framework
# (OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey, via X509_set_pubkey -> X509_PUBKEY_set),
# which grows an internal cache with realloc instead of a fresh malloc.
{
   openssl_internal_encoder_cache_still_reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:realloc
   ...
   fun:OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey
   ...
}

# The two suppressions above match by function-name prefix
# (fun:CRYPTO_malloc, fun:OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey). That is precise,
# but it covers only the exact call paths that reach those two functions. A
# `make memtest` run of this suite under heavy concurrent system load gives
# dozens more "still reachable" reports through entirely OTHER call chains
# inside libcrypto.so.3/libssl.so.3: OPENSSL_LH_insert via
# OSSL_LIB_CTX_get0_global_default, OPENSSL_sk_insert via
# OSSL_DECODER_do_all_provided/OSSL_ENCODER_do_all_provided, EVP_MD_fetch,
# CRYPTO_aligned_alloc and several more. Under that load the
# ctls_sni/ctls_alpn race-stress tests of this suite call
# ctls_ctx_cert_add()/ctls_ctx_alpn_add() -> _ctls_ctx_rebuild_locked() ->
# SSL_CTX_new_ex() over and over in a tight loop, and each call can fill a
# DIFFERENT lazily-initialized OpenSSL 3 provider/algorithm/OSSL_LIB_CTX
# cache the first time it reaches that particular internal code path. The
# backtraces show that each report comes entirely from the internals of
# libssl.so.3/libcrypto.so.3, with no application-code frame anywhere above
# the allocator, so it really starts from an internal OpenSSL call and not
# from a bug in ctls.c that happens to route through OpenSSL.
#
# The reports are real and come from this exact suite. `tests/ctls` passes
# clean when it runs alone (a single run or 3 concurrent ones), so this is a
# gap in the narrow function-name-prefix coverage of the two rules ABOVE that
# depends on load and scheduling, and not a new leak. Every one of these
# reports is in the same category that those two rules accept (an internal
# OpenSSL cache that lives for the whole process and is never freed on
# purpose), reached through a call path that their narrow prefixes don't
# cover. Instead of an open-ended list of more narrow function-name rules
# that keeps growing, the two rules below match on the ORIGIN LIBRARY of the
# allocation. Any "still reachable" block whose backtrace has at least one
# frame inside libcrypto.so.3 or libssl.so.3 is, by construction, memory
# that our code didn't allocate and can't safely free, whichever of
# OpenSSL's many internal call paths produced it. Freeing it would need
# OPENSSL_cleanup(), which isn't safe to call unconditionally in an
# embeddable library (see the reasoning at the top of this file).
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_via_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_via_libssl
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libssl.so.3
   ...
}

# glibc's own per-thread TLS bookkeeping (the "dtv", dynamic thread vector),
# which _dl_allocate_tls/allocate_dtv allocate as soon as pthread_create
# creates a new thread. Valgrind reports it as "possibly lost" and not as
# "still reachable" because its leak checker classifies an INTERIOR pointer
# (as opposed to a pointer to the exact start address of a block) that way;
# the valgrind manual defines "possibly lost". glibc caches a thread's dtv
# block on purpose and recycles it for a LATER pthread_create() call instead
# of freeing it once that thread exits or is joined. That makes it a
# well-known, harmless valgrind/glibc interaction, whether or not the thread
# that caused the allocation was joined properly, and this suite shows it:
# both of its own thread-creating tests,
# ctls_sni.cert_add_race_during_live_handshake_does_not_crash and
# ctls_alpn.alpn_add_race_during_live_handshake_does_not_crash, join their
# rotation thread unconditionally. The rule is scoped narrowly to this exact
# allocation shape (it is not a broad libc-wide suppression), because it is
# a different category from the OpenSSL-internal one above, and one that is
# well documented on its own.
{
   glibc_thread_tls_dtv_possibly_lost
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:calloc
   fun:calloc
   fun:allocate_dtv
   fun:_dl_allocate_tls
   ...
   fun:pthread_create@@*
   ...
}

# There is deliberately no rule here for our own code.
# _ctls_ctx_rebuild_locked() in ctls.c walks the named/SNI certificates with
# ccol_chmap_walk_next(),
# which allocates nothing, so the rebuild has no iterator that could leak.
# _check_chmap_iter_balance_at_exit in tests/ctls/tests.c is a process-exit
# destructor that aborts as soon as any chashmap iterator of the process is
# left outstanding (chashmap_iter_outstanding_count_for_tests() in
# chashmap.c), so if a "possibly lost" report from our code ever shows up in
# `make memtest`, the real valgrind report stays visible and you can compare
# it with that exit-time check instead of having it hidden behind a rule.
